WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.905 --> 00:00:19.367
Nothing could bring back my dear friend Helen, who had died so young from tuberculosis.

2
00:00:20.408 --> 00:00:26.871
But after her death and the terrible typhus outbreak, an investigation was launched into conditions at Lowood.

3
00:00:27.871 --> 00:00:33.407
The unhealthy nature of the site, the poor quality of the food and water, the wretchedness of the

4
00:00:33.447 --> 00:00:38.410
girls' clothing and accommodations: all these things were discovered and deplored.

5
00:00:39.491 --> 00:00:43.874
Several wealthy individuals contributed money to improve the school, and Mr.

6
00:00:43.914 --> 00:00:46.536
Brocklehurst was stripped of most of his authority.

7
00:00:47.637 --> 00:00:52.080
In time Lowood became a truly useful and noble institution.

8
00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:57.884
I remained there for eight years, six as a student and two as a teacher.

9
00:00:58.936 --> 00:01:03.159
Through all the changes at Lowood, Miss Temple continued as superintendent.

10
00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.282
In my life she had taken the place of mother, governess, and friend.

11
00:01:09.943 --> 00:01:15.627
However, when I was 18, she married a clergyman and moved to a distant county.

12
00:01:16.668 --> 00:01:19.170
From the day she left, I was no longer the same.

13
00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:24.333
I'd absorbed from Miss Temple something of her nature and much of her habits.

14
00:01:25.514 --> 00:01:28.516
I was quiet, and I believed I was content.

15
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.645
But as soon as her carriage departed after the wedding, I began to feel the stirring of old

16
00:01:34.685 --> 00:01:40.570
emotions. Up in my room, I went to my window and looked out at the hilly horizon.

17
00:01:41.671 --> 00:01:45.334
I traced the winding road until it vanished between two mountains.

18
00:01:46.415 --> 00:01:48.657
How I longed to follow it farther!

19
00:01:48.677 --> 00:01:51.439
"Let me be useful elsewhere!"

20
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.281
I cried in desperation.

21
00:01:54.442 --> 00:01:58.990
Just then a bell called me to supper, and I was not free to resume my thoughts until

22
00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.492
bedtime. "What do I want?"

23
00:02:01.932 --> 00:02:03.914
I asked myself as I sat up in bed.

24
00:02:05.616 --> 00:02:06.736
And then I knew.

25
00:02:06.756 --> 00:02:10.259
"I'd like a new position in a new house.

26
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:12.941
How do people get new positions?

27
00:02:13.902 --> 00:02:17.045
They ask friends, I suppose, but I have no friends."

28
00:02:18.186 --> 00:02:21.749
Agitated from thinking so hard, I got up and paced the room.

29
00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:28.090
When I lay down again, the answer came to me: I must advertise in the newspaper.

30
00:02:29.091 --> 00:02:32.894
Before the bell rang the next morning, I had my advertisement written.

31
00:02:33.955 --> 00:02:40.300
It read as follows: "A young lady accustomed to teaching desires employment in a family with young children.

32
00:02:41.381 --> 00:02:46.345
She is qualified to teach the usual subjects plus French, drawing, and music.

33
00:02:47.365 --> 00:02:50.448
Reply to J.E., Post Office, Lowton."

34
00:02:51.588 --> 00:02:56.410
After tea I walked the two miles to Lowton and discreetly took my letter to the post office.

35
00:02:58.191 --> 00:03:00.712
I waited a week before I checked for a response.

36
00:03:00.732 --> 00:03:04.434
"Are there any letters for J.E.?"

37
00:03:04.854 --> 00:03:06.654
I anxiously asked the postmistress.

38
00:03:07.835 --> 00:03:13.257
She peered at me over her spectacles, and then she opened a drawer and fumbled among its contents

39
00:03:13.297 --> 00:03:15.678
for so long that I began to lose hope.

40
00:03:16.899 --> 00:03:19.580
At last she pushed an envelope across the counter.

41
00:03:20.688 --> 00:03:23.230
Outside the post office, I tore open the letter.

42
00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:25.251
It was from a Mrs.

43
00:03:25.351 --> 00:03:31.675
Fairfax of Thornfield near Millcote, and it offered a position teaching a little girl under ten years of

44
00:03:31.795 --> 00:03:34.637
age for the salary of thirty pounds per year.

45
00:03:35.717 --> 00:03:40.680
If I wanted the job, I should send my name, address, and letter of reference to Mrs.

46
00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:44.142
Fairfax. I studied the letter a long time.

47
00:03:45.163 --> 00:03:48.141
The writing was old fashioned, and I pictured Mrs.

48
00:03:48.181 --> 00:03:51.423
Fairfax as a model of elderly English respectability.

49
00:03:53.104 --> 00:03:57.887
Thornfield was doubtless the name of her house, a neat and orderly spot I was sure.

50
00:03:58.948 --> 00:04:04.531
I knew that Millcote was a large manufacturing town, seventy miles nearer London than Lowood.

51
00:04:05.572 --> 00:04:10.975
The next day I spoke to the superintendent at Lowood school about my opportunity and the letter of

52
00:04:11.036 --> 00:04:13.817
reference. She contacted Mr.

53
00:04:13.857 --> 00:04:15.959
Brocklehurst, who said that Mrs.

54
00:04:16.059 --> 00:04:18.841
Reed must be written to because she was my guardian.

55
00:04:19.862 --> 00:04:25.166
Mrs. Reed replied that I might do as I pleased as she'd long ago given up caring what

56
00:04:25.286 --> 00:04:25.626
I did.

57
00:04:26.747 --> 00:04:29.449
The school provided me with a reference, and Mrs.

58
00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:31.471
Fairfax offered me the position.

59
00:04:32.531 --> 00:04:35.153
I now busied myself preparing to leave Lowood.

60
00:04:36.214 --> 00:04:38.396
On my last day I packed my trunk.

61
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:43.903
In half an hour, the carrier was to take it to Lowton, where I was to meet the

62
00:04:43.943 --> 00:04:45.464
coach early the next morning.

63
00:04:47.165 --> 00:04:50.367
I was too excited to sit still, so I paced the halls.

64
00:04:51.468 --> 00:04:56.251
"Miss," said a servant who encountered me, "someone wants to see you downstairs."

65
00:04:57.332 --> 00:05:03.216
I hurried downstairs, and as I passed the back parlor, a woman ran out and clasped my hand.

66
00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:07.038
"Jane! I'd know you anywhere!"

67
00:05:08.259 --> 00:05:11.497
I studied the woman, who appeared to be a well-dressed servant.

68
00:05:11.517 --> 00:05:14.938
"Bessie!" I cried as I embraced her.

69
00:05:15.958 --> 00:05:21.480
We went into the parlor, where she explained that when my letter arrived at Gateshead, she had vowed

70
00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:23.240
to see me one last time.

71
00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:28.102
"Tell me everything about everyone at Gateshead," I implored her.

72
00:05:28.122 --> 00:05:35.144
"Miss Eliza is much taller than you are," Bessie began, "and Miss Georgiana is very handsome.

73
00:05:36.852 --> 00:05:40.274
You look like a lady, and I suppose that's all we can expect.

74
00:05:41.375 --> 00:05:43.156
You were no beauty as a child."

75
00:05:44.257 --> 00:05:45.757
I smiled at her frankness.

76
00:05:45.777 --> 00:05:49.099
"I suppose Georgiana is very pretty?"

77
00:05:49.119 --> 00:05:50.880
"Oh yes.

78
00:05:51.941 --> 00:05:55.103
She went to London last winter and everyone admired her.

79
00:05:56.204 --> 00:06:01.347
A nobleman fell in love with her, but his family was against the match, so he and Miss

80
00:06:01.387 --> 00:06:03.308
Georgiana decided to run away.

81
00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:07.022
But Miss Eliza found out and they were stopped.

82
00:06:08.102 --> 00:06:10.403
Now the young ladies fight like cats and dogs."

83
00:06:11.584 --> 00:06:12.965
"And what of John Reed?"

84
00:06:13.345 --> 00:06:13.905
I asked.

85
00:06:13.925 --> 00:06:17.988
"He was sent home from college for failing his classes.

86
00:06:19.088 --> 00:06:22.670
His mother is always worried about him because he spends too much money.

87
00:06:23.751 --> 00:06:25.132
Now tell me about yourself.

88
00:06:26.192 --> 00:06:27.333
Can you play the piano?"

89
00:06:28.314 --> 00:06:32.276
There was one in the room, so I played a waltz or two and she was charmed.

90
00:06:33.932 --> 00:06:34.713
"Can you draw?"

91
00:06:35.133 --> 00:06:35.694
she asked.

92
00:06:36.754 --> 00:06:41.378
I pointed to one of my paintings over the fireplace, and Bessie was impressed.

93
00:06:42.479 --> 00:06:46.042
Then she asked if I'd ever heard anything from my Eyre relatives.

94
00:06:47.183 --> 00:06:47.804
I had not.

95
00:06:48.824 --> 00:06:51.146
I was stunned when she told me that a Mr.

96
00:06:51.426 --> 00:06:54.789
Eyre had come looking for me at Gateshead seven years ago.

97
00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:59.253
Mrs. Reed told him that I was at school fifty miles away.

98
00:07:00.434 --> 00:07:03.328
A wine merchant on his way to Madeira, Mr.

99
00:07:03.448 --> 00:07:05.270
Eyre did not have time to find me.

100
00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:11.253
Bessie and I talked of old times for another hour, but I kept thinking of how Mrs.

101
00:07:11.333 --> 00:07:14.035
Reed had never written to me about Mr.

102
00:07:14.195 --> 00:07:20.299
Eyre. The next morning I saw Bessie again for a few minutes as we waited for our respective

103
00:07:20.359 --> 00:07:21.420
coaches in Lowton.

104
00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:27.283
Then I climbed into the vehicle that was to carry me to my new life in a strange

105
00:07:27.384 --> 00:07:27.684
place.
